  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 428 AM EST SAT NOV 7 2026\n\nThe long-term period begins Sunday night with a series of embedded\nshortwaves within the northern jet stream. Of relevance, a broad\nupper-level low will be positioned over Ontario, with an upstream\ntrough digging near the northern Rockies. Cyclonic flow around the\nCanadian low will maintain west-northwest flow aloft across eastern\nKentucky.\n\nConditions will remain dry across eastern Kentucky through Wednesday\nas high pressure dominates the region. Sunday and Monday nights,\ntemperatures are forecast to cool into the mid-30s, with colder spots\ndipping into the lower 30s. Recent NBM guidance has exhibited a cold\nbias, so temperatures were blended with higher-resolution models to\nnudge values upward, bringing the forecast back within the 25th to\n75th percentile range of overall guidance. Frost and localized freeze\nconditions will be most likely in these colder spots, while patchy\nriver valley fog will also be possible overnight.\n\nTemperatures will warm significantly Tuesday and Wednesday as\nupper-level heights rise and surface high pressure shifts across the\nSoutheast. Under mostly clear skies and light westerly flow aloft,\nefficient daytime heating within a dry air mass will support\nafternoon highs climbing into the mid to upper 60s both days (5-10\ndegrees above seasonal normals).\n\nThe pattern turns much colder and more unsettled late in the week. A\nvigorous northern-stream shortwave diving out of western Canada will\ncarve out a deep, anomalous longwave trough over the eastern third of\nthe CONUS. An attendant cold front will sweep through the\nCommonwealth late Wednesday into Thursday, bringing increasing clouds\nand increasing rain chances.\n\nThe core of the upper trough is modeled to move overhead on Friday,\npushing 500 mb heights below the 5th climatological percentile and\nushering in a brisk Canadian air mass on breezy northwest winds.\nHighs Friday and Saturday may struggle to climb out of the 40s, with\novernight lows potentially dropping into the upper 20s and lower 30s.\nWith model soundings suggesting steep low-level lapse rates alongside\nnorthwest upslope flow, lingering showers could mix with or\ntransition to the first flurries of the season across the higher\nterrain of southeast Kentucky Thursday night into Friday before\nsurface high pressure restores dry conditions for the weekend.",
